1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:03,520
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:04,200
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:09,800
My name is Catherine and we've got another lesson in our series about small talk today.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:10,520
That's right.

5
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:19,160
Today we are at the office again and we're going to be talking very briefly about something, a business meeting that we're going to have soon.

6
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:20,160
That's right.

7
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:21,680
So let's check out our dialogue.

8
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:22,980
We'll be back in a moment.

9
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:30,270
Ah, Michelle.

10
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:32,350
Hi, I was hoping to see you.

11
00:00:32,630 --> 00:00:33,150
How have you been?

12
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:33,890
How's the family?

13
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:35,710
Oh, hello, Mr. Campbell.

14
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:38,010
I'm fine and Jack's doing well.

15
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:39,010
How are you?

16
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:39,850
I'm fine.

17
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:40,450
Thanks.

18
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:42,150
I got your report this morning.

19
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:43,230
Thanks for that.

20
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:44,630
Are you joining the conference today?

21
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,200
Yes, I'm leaving at 4 p.m.

22
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,200
Good.

23
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,050
Well, we can discuss this more then.

24
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,030
But I think the figures are looking very good for this quarter.

25
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:53,090
Yes, me too.

26
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,930
I'm planning to discuss the advertising budget at the conference.

27
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:58,270
I don't think we should continue with the TV advertising.

28
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:00,150
No, me neither.

29
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:01,570
It's far too expensive.

30
00:01:02,230 --> 00:01:04,050
Well, let's discuss this more at the conference.

31
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:05,710
Maybe we can share a taxi there?

32
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:06,530
Yeah, sure.

33
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:17,520
All right, we're back, so now let's take a look at some vocab on Language Takeaway.

34
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:18,160
Language Takeaway.

35
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:24,750
Today in Language Takeaway we have a number of phrases and words about business.

36
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:28,490
The first one we have is, are you joining the conference today?

37
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:29,270
That's right.

38
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:33,350
So we're going to go to a conference, actually, which is a little bit different than a meeting.

39
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:41,710
That's right. A meeting is something you normally do with people at your own company. But a conference is when people from many different companies meet.

40
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:47,210
That's right. And also, I think you don't really have too much input at a conference.

41
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:52,020
You mostly listen to other people speak about a topic or a subject.

42
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:52,820
That's right.

43
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:56,640
And I think these are normally held in conference centers or hotels.

44
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:58,740
You have a nice lunch.

45
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:00,560
So they're pretty big deals.

46
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,280
That's right. All right, so it's more of a formal business conference that we're going to.

47
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:12,300
And well, they're actually going to discuss different aspects of the company as well, especially the figures.

48
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:13,200
All right.

49
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,760
So in the context of business, the word figure actually means number.

50
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:24,980
Okay, so we can say the sales numbers for this year are a kind of figure.

51
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:26,540
That's right.

52
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:30,060
So we're talking about money, basically, when we're talking about numbers.

53
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:35,560
That's right. So when the figures look good, it means that our income, our revenue, this looks good.

54
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:42,200
That's right. And when we usually talk about income or revenue, we usually have to separate it into time.

55
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,000
And here in the dialogue, we're talking about a quarter.

56
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:56,040
All right, so quarter, Q-U, quarter, is a period of time, very common in business, that is a quarter of the year.

57
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:57,500
So three months.

58
00:02:57,900 --> 00:02:58,760
Three months, that's right.

59
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:02,040
January, February, March, April, May, June, and so on.

60
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:04,080
That's right. So you have four quarters.

61
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:08,880
And that's actually what, that's why you call it a quarter because it's one fourth.

62
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:17,180
So we can say the figures for the first quarter were very good, but unfortunately, the figures for the second quarter were very bad.

63
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:18,000
That's right.

64
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:23,420
And actually, another similar way that you use quarter is the 25 cent coin.

65
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:26,330
It's called a quarter because it's a quarter of a dollar.

66
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:31,150
That's right. And if you're in America, you know that it has an eagle, a bird on one side of it.

67
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:31,790
That's right.

68
00:03:32,490 --> 00:03:36,190
All right, so we are taking a look at the figures for the quarter.

69
00:03:36,890 --> 00:03:43,010
And they're saying, well, it's not looking too good because we need to talk about the advertising budget.

70
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:44,080
All right.

71
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:47,900
So this is something that you think is very important.

72
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:48,680
It's budget.

73
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:51,500
It's how much money you have to spend on something.

74
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,320
And in this case, you're spending it on advertising.

75
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:56,920
So this is ads that you make.

76
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:57,680
That's right.

77
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,600
So as you said, a budget is the amount of money that you have available to spend on something.

78
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,560
And on this occasion, we're talking about advertising.

79
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,980
Now, you can have a budget for different things, right?

80
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:11,580
You could have a marketing budget.

81
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:13,200
So the money you have for marketing.

82
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:17,980
You could have a personnel budget, which is the money you would set aside for people.

83
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:24,100
So how much you pay your employees. Right. So you can pretty much mix up many different nouns with budget.

84
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,420
Even you can have a budget for your home.

85
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:27,670
How much you spend at home.

86
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,030
That's right. So many people have this for their families.

87
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:35,590
They say this month I will spend $100 on shopping and $200 on shoes.

88
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,800
So it's like a family budget, you would call it.

89
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:44,510
That's right. Okay. So that's all the words we have for you on language takeaway.

90
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:48,570
Let's review everything quickly and we'll be back in a bit with Fluency Builder.

